{"context_update": {"set": {"turn_control": "Total limit: 100 submissions including questions and unsuccessful finals. Used question-equivalents: 98. At slot 100, you MUST submit a final explanation."}, "remove": [], "append_visible_history": [{"type": "final", "text": "The man was being blackmailed and had to deliver a ransom, with a hostage to be exchanged. The extortionists had named a sum substantially larger than one million yen. He could raise only about one million yen of cash, then added cash of his own, so the bag held more than one million yen but still less than the full demand.

His central worry was for his own safety: he feared that if the extortionists discovered the bag contained exactly one million yen—the precise, recognizable sum that would reveal he had not properly met their much larger demand—they would turn on him. Simply handing over one million yen would therefore be dangerous.

By adding extra cash, he made the bag's total no longer exactly one million yen. The fuller pile of cash was meant to look consistent with the much larger sum they demanded, so that at the exchange the extortionists would believe they had received the full amount and would not scrutinize or count it closely. Even though the bag was actually still short, that mistaken belief was what he needed to get through the handover safely. The money, including the cash he had added, was handed over for good; he was trading a bit more money for his own protection.", "feedback": "no"}]}}

